Tags are in and work at both 1280px and 375px. The final run passed 61 unit tests and 32 browser tests in real Chrome; type checking, the linter and the production build are clean. Nothing is committed yet.

**How it works**
- **What counts as a tag:** `#` followed by a letter, then letters, digits, `_`, or `-` / `/` between parts (`#todo`, `#work-items`, `#project/alpha`). It must start a word. So `# Heading`, `#12`, `C#sharp`, `https://x.com/#frag` and `[x](#anchor)` are not tags, and nothing inside code blocks or `` `inline code` `` is either. Tags ignore case and are shown in lower case.
- **Sidebar:** a collapsible "Tags" section lists every tag A–Z with the number of notes using it. Clicking a tag filters the list, clicking it again clears the filter, and the filter combines with search.
- **Preview:** tags show as chips. Clicking one also filters the list; on mobile it takes you back to the list.
- **Suggestions while typing `#`:** a list of existing tags opens under the `#`, or above it near the bottom of the editor. Arrow keys move through it, Enter or Tab inserts the chosen tag, a click inserts too, and Escape closes it. Ctrl+Z undoes an insert.
  - The exact tag you've already typed isn't offered, so Enter after a finished tag still makes a new line.
  - Nothing is suggested inside code blocks or after `# ` (a heading).
- **Rename:** the ✎ next to a tag edits its name in place. Enter saves and rewrites that tag in every note, in any capitalisation. Similar tags (`#work-items` when renaming `#work`) and code are left alone. Escape cancels, and an invalid name shows an error and changes nothing. If the tag was the active filter, the filter follows the new name.

**Decisions for you to check**
- **A tag counts once you finish the word.** Without this, typing `#work` made `#w`, `#wo`, `#wor` flicker through the sidebar. Typing past the word or leaving the editor adds it. The downside: on desktop, a tag doesn't appear in the sidebar until you move off it.
- **Renaming counts as an edit,** so the notes it changed move to the top of the list. That's consistent with "sorted by last edited", but it can feel surprising after a big rename. I can make renames keep notes in place if you prefer.
- **Renaming onto a tag that already exists merges the two.** There's no warning before it happens.
- **The welcome note now has a `#welcome` tag,** so new users see the feature straight away.

The throwaway test file in `e2e/tmp/` is still there from earlier because deleting it needs your approval.

**What I tested and how**
- **Logic (`npm test`, Vitest, 22 new tests, 61 in total):**
  - which text counts as a tag and which doesn't (headings, numbers, mid-word, links, code, other alphabets);
  - counting notes per tag rather than mentions;
  - checking rename names, and renames handling capitalisation, whole tags only, code and Windows line endings;
  - spotting a `#` being typed, including inside code;
  - ordering suggestions and inserting the chosen one;
  - tag chips in the preview.
- **Real browser (`npx playwright test e2e/tags.spec.ts e2e/notes.spec.ts`, Chrome, 7 new tests each at 1280×800 and 375×740):**
  - tag counts in the sidebar, and that editing a note updates them;
  - filter on and off, switching tags, and filter plus search;
  - suggestions with Enter, arrow keys, Escape then Enter for a new line, clicking an option, inserting mid-text, and Ctrl+Z;
  - the suggestion list staying on screen at 375px;
  - rename with an invalid name, a capitalisation variant, a similar tag, a code block, cancel, merge, and a reload afterwards;
  - clicking a chip in the preview;
  - that a half-typed tag isn't counted.

  All 16 earlier tests still pass.
- **By eye:** I looked at screenshots of the suggestion list, the filtered list, the rename error, the renamed tags and the preview chips at both sizes, plus dark mode. That's how I caught the half-typed tag in the sidebar and the stray `#tag` in the welcome note, and fixed both.
